          "type": "turn",
          "question": "type 1 here, been sick all day, meter says 24 and the ketone strip went dark purple, my breath smells odd",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now, or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait.** Being sick all day, a high meter reading, large ketones and an unusual breath smell together are reasons for emergency care. A type 1 diabetic with these signs needs to be checked urgently for diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), which can become life-threatening.\n\nThe automatic urgency check did not match a red-flag pattern, and that is not a clearance. The guidance in \"Diabetic emergencies: very high and very low blood sugar\" says that vomiting or abdominal pain, and breath with a fruity or acetone smell, call for emergency services immediately. It also says that rapid breathing and weakness in someone with type 1 diabetes, or anyone unable to keep fluids down, need the same response. Please follow that guidance.\n\nThe meter unit is unclear. A reading of 24 is dangerous whether it is mg/dL or mmol/L, so it needs immediate attention either way.\n\nWatch for these warning signs and tell the emergency team if you notice them:\n- Vomiting, or being unable to keep fluids down\n- Stomach or abdominal pain\n- Fast, deep or laboured breathing\n- Confusion, drowsiness, or difficulty staying awake\n- Severe thirst, a very dry mouth, or not passing urine\n\nDo not drive yourself. Call emergency services, or ask someone to take you right away. If you become drowsy or confused, or cannot stay awake, someone should call emergency services for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
